WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.560 --> 00:00:06.620
This episode was supported by Brilliant.org

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:07.580
Hey Crazies.

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:10.380
It’s time to tackle my favorite paradox
of all time.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:12.120
The Twins Paradox!

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.700
Unfortunately, I don’t have any twins, even
though everyone keeps asking.

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:17.500
But I do have clones.

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.820
I’d like to introduce you to Rocket Clone.

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:20.560
Hi!!

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:22.800
He’s super useful for this sort of thing.

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.540
To infinity and beyond!

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:26.360
Yeah yeah, whatever. Just get in the rocket.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:27.679
Ok, let’s do this!

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.260
Rocket Clone zips off at 55 percent the speed of
light to, oh, I don’t know.

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:36.740
Let’s say Wolf 359, which is a modest 8
light years away.

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:40.309
By the way, for those of you who don’t know,
a light year is not a time.

00:00:40.309 --> 00:00:42.720
It’s a speed multiplied by a time.

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:44.360
A light year is a distance.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:48.980
Anyway, Rocket Clone gets to Wolf 359 and
immediately turns around.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.160
When he finally gets back to the station,
29 years have gone by for me,

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:55.899
but only 24 years have gone by for him.

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.640
If you’re familiar with relativity, this
isn’t that surprising.

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:05.100
Rule #2 for Relativity says: Every observer
carries their own set of coordinates

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.440
and their own clock.

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:07.520
I get a clock.

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:08.540
You get a clock.

00:01:08.540 --> 00:01:10.280
Everybody gets a clock!!!

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:15.480
Different observers traveling different paths
through space-time are going to measure different

00:01:15.480 --> 00:01:16.480
amounts of time.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.840
There’s no paradox there.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:22.060
A quick way to calculate the difference is
by using something called the gamma factor.

00:01:22.060 --> 00:01:26.340
It’s a number that shows up all over relativity,
so get used to seeing it.

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:31.180
Using that factor, I expected Rocket Clone
to be only 24 years older,

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:33.420
even though I’m 29 years older.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.110
There’s no surprise on my end.

00:01:35.110 --> 00:01:37.400
Rocket Clone is the one that’s surprised.

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:43.360
Rule #4 says: It will always look to an observer
as though they are stationary in space.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.420
As far as Rocket Clone is concerned, he stayed
in one place and I’m the one that moved.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:53.810
We also have Rule #1: No point of view has
preference over any other point of view.

00:01:53.810 --> 00:01:56.200
Rocket Clone is the one with the paradox.

00:01:56.200 --> 00:02:00.539
He aged 24 years and if he does the same calculation
I did,

00:02:00.540 --> 00:02:05.460
he expects to find me only 20 years older,
not 29 years older.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.380
He expects to be the older one.

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:08.600
Paradox!!

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.720
Ahhhhh!!!!!!!!

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:13.120
Huh...

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:14.540
Not sure why that happened.

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:16.880
I’ve said it before and I’ll say it again.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.520
Paradoxes are not a problem of the universe.

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:21.700
They’re a problem of the human mind.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.600
The universe doesn’t have a problem.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:24.600
We do.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:28.140
When it comes to paradoxes, there are only
two ways we could have made a mistake:

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.140
Option One!

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:31.320
We’ve made an assumption we shouldn’t
have made.

00:02:31.320 --> 00:02:34.640
Meaning one of the rules we used isn’t actually
a rule.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.640
It’s difficult to argue with Rule #4 since
perception is pretty self-centered

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:42.980
and Rule #2 can’t be the problem since that’s
what relativity is all about.

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:46.800
That leaves only Rule #1, so let’s take
a closer look at that.

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:51.420
Throwing out rule #1 is a very common way
people try to solve this paradox,

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.200
but that unravels the entirety of relativity.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.340
I mean, do you think I would have spent an
entire video giving you a list of rules for

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:00.900
relativity that could break in relativity?

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:02.100
Don’t be ridiculous!

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.180
It’s rule #1 for crying out loud!

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:06.960
Anyway, here’s how the typical explanation
goes:

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.120
Since Rocket Clone accelerates, he feels a
force toward the back of his rocket.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:13.920
A force of uniform gravity to be specific.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.780
This breaks the symmetry between his clock
and mine

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:19.360
somehow making mine the correct clock.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:21.200
But here’s the problem with that solution.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.540
There never was any symmetry between him and
me to begin with.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.880
Like we said earlier:
Different observers traveling different paths

00:03:27.890 --> 00:03:30.010
through space-time
are going to measure different amounts of

00:03:30.010 --> 00:03:30.740
time.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.500
And that’s not just true of time.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:37.040
It’s also true of distance, length, and
a whole list of other measurements.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:41.000
Having one of the observers accelerate just
adds another measurement to the list of things

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:42.300
they disagree on.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:43.880
Forces are now on the list.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:45.920
That’s not that significant of a change.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.200
It certainly doesn’t make one person right
and another person wrong.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.060
It just makes them more relative.

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:54.510
But don’t accelerated points of view require
general relativity?

00:03:54.510 --> 00:03:59.510
Yes, but general relativity fixed that by
strengthening rule #1,

00:03:59.510 --> 00:04:01.000
not by throwing it away.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:06.780
Rule #1 holds true in general relativity just
as much as it does in special relativity.

00:04:06.780 --> 00:04:10.180
The fact that Rocket Clone accelerates isn’t
the problem.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.780
Then what other mistake could we have made?

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:13.460
I’m so glad you asked!

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:14.500
Option Two!

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:16.900
We didn’t consider something we should have
considered.

00:04:16.900 --> 00:04:20.180
I feel like there’s something missing here.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:21.560
1

00:04:21.560 --> 00:04:23.320
2

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.340
4

00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:27.420
Oh! It’s Rule #3. We forgot Rule #3.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:30.500
We have to use the correct coordinate transformation.

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:33.100
Remember this quick calculation Rocket Clone
did?

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:34.100
It’s wrong.

00:04:34.100 --> 00:04:35.880
And this quick calculation I did?

00:04:35.880 --> 00:04:37.240
Also wrong!

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.060
But didn’t you get the right answer?

00:04:39.060 --> 00:04:40.760
Yeah, but that’s, well,

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.860
OK, maybe “wrong” was a bit harsh.

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:44.900
Let’s go with “sloppy”?

00:04:44.900 --> 00:04:49.140
When we do this, we get no perspective on
what we’re calculating.

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:52.520
The number Rocket Clone calculated is valid.

00:04:52.520 --> 00:04:55.620
It’s just not the answer to the question
he was asking.

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:58.480
You’re not always calculating what you think
you’re calculating.

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:02.940
It’s safer to use the full transformations
or, better yet, written in matrix form

00:05:02.940 --> 00:05:06.680
because you transform both space and time
together.

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:08.700
It gives you a better idea of what you’re
looking at.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:10.440
But you know what works even better?

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.940
A space-time diagram!

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.820
If you’re not familiar with those, you should
go watch this video first.

00:05:14.830 --> 00:05:17.510
It’ll also help with all these rules I’ve
been giving you.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:21.730
So, in the Twins Paradox, the two paths look
like this.

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.510
Rocket Clone accelerates away from the space
station

00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:28.440
reaches 55 percent the speed of light and stops accelerating.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.740
He eventually gets to Wolf 359, turns around,
and the whole thing happens in reverse.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:36.280
The straight paths are constant velocity.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.740
The curved paths are the accelerations.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:43.340
So, yes, there are three sections where Rocket
Clone accelerates.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:44.780
But like I said before:

00:05:44.780 --> 00:05:48.320
The fact that Rocket Clone accelerates isn’t the problem.

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:51.260
This paradox does not require accelerations.

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:56.140
It persists even if Rocket Clone goes the
same velocity the whole time.

00:05:56.140 --> 00:06:01.380
These are the lengths of the two space-time
paths: 29 years and 24 years

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:04.120
If we’re going to remove all the accelerations
though,

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:07.020
then we can’t even let Rocket Clone turn
around.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:12.640
That means these are the numbers we expect
to see: 14.5 years and 12 years

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:14.920
because we’re only looking at half the trip.

00:06:14.920 --> 00:06:17.440
OK, now let’s look at the simplified diagram.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.680
Say Rocket Clone is already going at 55% the
speed of light

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.880
when he flies past my space station.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:26.020
Sometime later, he passes Wolf 359.

00:06:26.020 --> 00:06:28.699
He just keeps going because no accelerations.

00:06:28.700 --> 00:06:32.660
I say Rocket Clone’s path is about 14 and
half years tall,

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:35.180
but he says it’s about 12 years tall.

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:39.740
Exactly half the time differences from earlier
because this is half the trip.

00:06:39.740 --> 00:06:42.740
So this is totally consistent with everything
we got before.

00:06:42.740 --> 00:06:45.960
The paradox arose when tried to look the other
way.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:49.430
Starting from Rocket Clone’s coordinates,
we can look back at me and

00:06:49.430 --> 00:06:50.430
Wait a minute!!

00:06:50.430 --> 00:06:52.600
We’re not looking at Rocket Clone’s path
anymore.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:53.860
We’re looking at mine.

00:06:53.860 --> 00:06:56.220
That’s a completely different space-time
path.

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:57.699
Hmm.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:02.440
Rocket Clone’s path measured in Rocket Clone’s
coordinates is 12 years tall,

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.180
but that only includes a portion of my path.

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:08.480
A portion that’s about 10 years tall in
my coordinates.

00:07:08.480 --> 00:07:12.720
Exactly half the time difference from earlier
because this is half the trip.

00:07:12.720 --> 00:07:15.840
But that doesn’t actually say anything about
my path through space-time.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.750
It’s only saying things about his path,
which isn’t the question he was asking.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:24.180
The event of Rocket Clone passing Wolf 359
isn’t on my path.

00:07:24.180 --> 00:07:25.540
It’s on his.

00:07:25.540 --> 00:07:28.300
To line that up with the event at the end
of my path,

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:35.270
Rocket Clone has to wait over 5 more years
because my entire path is almost 17 and half

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:37.480
years tall in his coordinates.

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:41.020
These "horizontal" gridlines are lines of equal
time.

00:07:41.020 --> 00:07:44.740
That 17 and half years is the number Rocket
Clone should have used

00:07:44.740 --> 00:07:48.640
to calculate my time using that sloppy calculation
from earlier.

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:51.020
Except, he wouldn’t have known to do that.

00:07:51.020 --> 00:07:55.620
That extra 5 years doesn’t even exist in
the full Twins Paradox because, by then

00:07:55.630 --> 00:07:57.740
Rocket Clone would have turned around.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.740
He wouldn’t even be on that path anymore.

00:07:59.740 --> 00:08:01.340
So, here’s the real problem.

00:08:01.340 --> 00:08:07.060
Both of us are trying to calculate the location
and time of an event on someone else’s path

00:08:07.060 --> 00:08:08.900
in someone else’s coordinates.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:11.199
There are four different measurements here.

00:08:11.200 --> 00:08:14.420
Two different paths measured in two different
coordinates.

00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:18.780
Everyone agrees if those measurements are
at the same place and the same time

00:08:18.780 --> 00:08:21.100
because we just take the measurement together.

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.860
But, if they’re at different places, we
have a problem.

00:08:23.860 --> 00:08:29.000
At distant locations, those calculations are
just predictions, not measurements.

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:31.300
Each of us has to take a measurement of ourselves,

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.600
maybe one selfie every day,

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:35.400
and then send it to the other person.

00:08:35.400 --> 00:08:38.220
And that’s a whole other can of worms that
I’d rather not open.

00:08:38.220 --> 00:08:42.640
My point is this: The acceleration doesn’t
make one of time measurements incorrect.

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:47.390
It’s just that, without the acceleration,
we only get one shared event.

00:08:47.390 --> 00:08:50.779
The acceleration gives us a second shared
event.

00:08:50.779 --> 00:08:53.420
If we don’t want to deal with sending selfies
to each other

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.720
we need two shared events to measure the passage
of time.

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:59.620
One at the beginning
and one at the end.

00:08:59.630 --> 00:09:02.930
We can’t agree on any other events we might
want to include,

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:04.960
so we can’t compare measurements between
them.

00:09:04.960 --> 00:09:07.480
The acceleration doesn’t give anyone priority.

00:09:07.480 --> 00:09:09.640
It just fixes a measurement problem.

00:09:09.640 --> 00:09:13.800
You don’t have to throw out rule #1
if you do rule #3 correctly.

00:09:13.800 --> 00:09:19.060
The Twins Paradox disappears entirely if
you remember all the rules of relativity.

00:09:19.060 --> 00:09:21.200
So, are you satisfied with this solution?

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:22.720
Please discuss in the comments.

